# Rank 1455 / CVE-2022-0235: node-fetch follows a 3xx itself, and at 2.6.6 the
# request it builds for the next hop starts from `new Headers(request.headers)` --
# a verbatim copy of the header set the caller attached to the first request. The
# 3xx is written by the server that first request reached, so that server chooses
# the next host, and the copy hands it whatever `Authorization`, `Cookie`,
# `Cookie2` or `WWW-Authenticate` the caller meant for the original host. No
# injection is involved: the URL fetched is exactly the one the client is told to
# fetch. CWE-200.
#
# The fixed revision (1ef4b560a17e644a02a3bfdea7631ffeee578b35, a clean backport of #1449 onto
# the 2.x line; plain `git show` reads it and the parent is fully vulnerable) adds
# `isDomainOrSubdomain(request.url, locationURL)` and, when it is false, deletes
# `authorization`, `www-authenticate`, `cookie` and `cookie2` from the copied
# header set. It also moves `Location` resolution from `url.resolve` to the WHATWG
# `URL` parser, which is a correctness change carried in the same commit and not
# the security control. The regression tests it adds -- "should not forward secure
# headers to 3th party" and "should forward secure headers to same host" -- state
# the rule directly.
#
# Vocabulary. Nothing in the ontology said this. `code.UrlPolicyNotReappliedOnRedirect`
# (VYQL-RF-048) is the nearest neighbour and is the wrong reading: it is CWE-918,
# its `vulnerableTo` is `request_forgery.Ssrf`, and its review text asks whether a
# *URL policy* survives the hop. node-fetch has no URL policy to lose and the
# fetched host is not the problem -- the caller's credentials are. Reusing it
# would file a credential leak as server-side request forgery. `code.ExternalLinkReferrerLeak`
# is the same cross-origin exposure family but is an HTML `rel=noreferrer` fact on
# the Referer header; `code.HopByHopHeaderAuthBypass` is a proxy adding privileged
# headers rather than a client keeping the caller's; `code.CredentialAttributeLogExposure`
# and `code.KubernetesCurlAuthorizationHeaderLogLeak` are leaks into logs;
# `core.RedirectAllowlist` and `code.RedirectTargetSink` are the server-side open
# redirect. So `code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcrossRedirect` is minted, with
# VYQL-SEC-052, for the class: a redirect follower that carries the previous
# request's credential headers into a hop that may change host.
#
# The binding reads a redirect-following context as three facts that hold for any
# client rather than for this one: a `Location` value read, a request-options
# object being assembled with a `headers` property, and a redirect budget on that
# same object (`follow`, `counter`, `maxRedirects`, `redirectCount`, ...). The
# budget is what makes it a follower -- RFC 7231 requires the chain to be bounded,
# so every implementation carries such a field and a test asserting on a 3xx does
# not. The negation is on the credential header *names*, which the HTTP
# specifications fix, and not on the host comparison, whose helper the fixed revision is free
# to name anything; the names are read off both the literal and the
# regular-expression tokens, since a strip is written either as a list of quoted
# names or as a header-name pattern.
#
# Discrimination, three `--cache off` scans of `src/` at each revision, identical
# every run: 3 VYQL-SEC-052 at 1ef4b560^ and 0 at 1ef4b560. The three are one
# weakness: a JavaScript function context inherits the tokens of the functions
# nested inside it, so the `req.on('response', ...)` handler, the promise executor
# around it and the exported `fetch` all carry the same facts. `src/` totals 14
# findings at both revisions without this change and 17 / 14 with it.
#
# Hostile variants, all measured:
#   - the patched revision with `isDomainOrSubdomain` renamed to `sameSite`     -> 0
#     (nothing keys on the fixed revision's own vocabulary; the header names carry it)
#   - the vulnerable revision with a comment naming Authorization and Cookie
#     above the copy                                                            -> 3
#     (JS `literal:` tokens are AST-derived, so prose cannot silence the flag)
#   - the vulnerable revision with the host comparison added but no header
#     deleted                                                                   -> 3
#     (a comparison alone is not the control, and it has no vocabulary of its own)
#   - a follow-redirects-shaped hop builder sharing no vocabulary with node-fetch,
#     with and without `removeMatchingHeaders(/^(?:(?:proxy-)?authorization|cookie2?)$/i,
#     ...)`                                                                  -> 0 / 1
#     (the same weakness read on code written independently of this one)
#
# Known recall hole, stated in the binding as well: a follower whose strip list is
# a module-level constant is still reported, because a function context carries the
# tokens of the functions nested inside it and not those of the module around it.
#
# Collateral, over the ~1100 clones under /tmp/cve-work: 218 JavaScript/TypeScript
# files mention a `location` string literal beside a response status, 160 of them
# also assign `headers`, 93 distinct by content. A first draft keyed on
# `Location` + a status selector + a `headers` property reported 56 flags across
# 21 of those files -- next.js integration tests reading `res.headers.get('location')`
# after a 307, apollo-server's test suite, misskey's test helpers, next.js's own
# router-server. Adding the redirect-budget clause takes that to 1 flag on 1 file:
# livehelperchat's minified `react.app.ie.js`, where the whole bundle is one line
# and axios's `mergeConfig` contributes `maxRedirects` to the same inherited token
# set as an unrelated `location` literal. One bundle-wide artifact is the residual
# the binding's comment records. (One file, next.js's `compiled/webpack/bundle5.js`,
# exceeded a 240s bound and is not counted either way.)
test "rank1455: a redirect follower copying the previous request's headers into the next hop"
  lang javascript
  file src/index.js
  expect VYQL-SEC-052
  expect_review code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcrossRedirect
  code
  ```
  import http from 'http';
  import https from 'https';
  import Url from 'url';

  import Request, { getNodeRequestOptions } from './request';
  import Headers, { createHeadersLenient } from './headers';
  import FetchError from './fetch-error';

  const resolve_url = Url.resolve;

  export default function fetch(url, opts) {
  	return new fetch.Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  		const request = new Request(url, opts);
  		const options = getNodeRequestOptions(request);
  		const send = (options.protocol === 'https:' ? https : http).request;
  		const req = send(options);

  		req.on('response', res => {
  			const headers = createHeadersLenient(res.headers);

  			if (fetch.isRedirect(res.statusCode)) {
  				const location = headers.get('Location');
  				const locationURL = location === null ? null : resolve_url(request.url, location);

  				switch (request.redirect) {
  					case 'error':
  						reject(new FetchError(`redirect mode is set to error: ${request.url}`, 'no-redirect'));
  						return;
  					case 'follow':
  						if (locationURL === null) {
  							break;
  						}

  						if (request.counter >= request.follow) {
  							reject(new FetchError(`maximum redirect reached at: ${request.url}`, 'max-redirect'));
  							return;
  						}

  						const requestOpts = {
  							headers: new Headers(request.headers),
  							follow: request.follow,
  							counter: request.counter + 1,
  							agent: request.agent,
  							method: request.method,
  							body: request.body,
  							size: request.size
  						};

  						if (res.statusCode === 303) {
  							requestOpts.method = 'GET';
  							requestOpts.body = undefined;
  							requestOpts.headers.delete('content-length');
  						}

  						resolve(fetch(new Request(locationURL, requestOpts)));
  						return;
  				}
  			}
  		});
  	});
  }

  fetch.isRedirect = code => code === 301 || code === 302 || code === 303 || code === 307 || code === 308;
  ```

test "rank1455: the same follower dropping the credential headers when the hop changes host"
  lang javascript
  file src/index.js
  reject VYQL-SEC-052
  reject_review code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcrossRedirect
  code
  ```
  import http from 'http';
  import https from 'https';
  import Url from 'url';
  import whatwgUrl from 'whatwg-url';

  import Request, { getNodeRequestOptions } from './request.js';
  import Headers, { createHeadersLenient } from './headers.js';
  import FetchError from './fetch-error.js';

  const URL = Url.URL || whatwgUrl.URL;

  const isDomainOrSubdomain = (destination, original) => {
  	const orig = new URL(original).hostname;
  	const dest = new URL(destination).hostname;

  	return orig === dest || (orig[orig.length - dest.length - 1] === '.' && orig.endsWith(dest));
  };

  export default function fetch(url, opts) {
  	return new fetch.Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  		const request = new Request(url, opts);
  		const options = getNodeRequestOptions(request);
  		const send = (options.protocol === 'https:' ? https : http).request;
  		const req = send(options);

  		req.on('response', res => {
  			const headers = createHeadersLenient(res.headers);

  			if (fetch.isRedirect(res.statusCode)) {
  				const location = headers.get('Location');
  				let locationURL = null;
  				try {
  					locationURL = location === null ? null : new URL(location, request.url).toString();
  				} catch (err) {
  					if (request.redirect !== 'manual') {
  						reject(new FetchError(`uri requested responds with an invalid redirect URL: ${location}`, 'invalid-redirect'));
  						return;
  					}
  				}

  				switch (request.redirect) {
  					case 'error':
  						reject(new FetchError(`redirect mode is set to error: ${request.url}`, 'no-redirect'));
  						return;
  					case 'follow':
  						if (locationURL === null) {
  							break;
  						}

  						if (request.counter >= request.follow) {
  							reject(new FetchError(`maximum redirect reached at: ${request.url}`, 'max-redirect'));
  							return;
  						}

  						const requestOpts = {
  							headers: new Headers(request.headers),
  							follow: request.follow,
  							counter: request.counter + 1,
  							agent: request.agent,
  							method: request.method,
  							body: request.body,
  							size: request.size
  						};

  						if (!isDomainOrSubdomain(request.url, locationURL)) {
  							for (const name of ['authorization', 'www-authenticate', 'cookie', 'cookie2']) {
  								requestOpts.headers.delete(name);
  							}
  						}

  						if (res.statusCode === 303) {
  							requestOpts.method = 'GET';
  							requestOpts.body = undefined;
  							requestOpts.headers.delete('content-length');
  						}

  						resolve(fetch(new Request(locationURL, requestOpts)));
  						return;
  				}
  			}
  		});
  	});
  }

  fetch.isRedirect = code => code === 301 || code === 302 || code === 303 || code === 307 || code === 308;
  ```

test "rank1455: an independently spelled credential-header strip is the same control"
  lang javascript
  file lib/follow.js
  reject VYQL-SEC-052
  reject_review code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcrossRedirect
  code
  ```
  const { URL } = require('url');

  function nextHop(res, previous) {
  	if (res.statusCode < 300 || res.statusCode > 399) {
  		return null;
  	}

  	if (previous.redirectCount >= previous.maxRedirects) {
  		throw new Error('too many redirects');
  	}

  	const target = new URL(res.headers['location'], previous.url);
  	const carried = Object.assign({}, previous.headers);

  	if (new URL(previous.url).host !== target.host) {
  		for (const name of ['Authorization', 'Cookie', 'Cookie2', 'WWW-Authenticate', 'Proxy-Authorization']) {
  			delete carried[name.toLowerCase()];
  		}
  	}

  	return {
  		url: target.toString(),
  		headers: carried,
  		method: previous.method,
  		maxRedirects: previous.maxRedirects,
  		redirectCount: previous.redirectCount + 1
  	};
  }

  module.exports = { nextHop };
  ```

test "rank1455: the same strip written as a header-name pattern is the same control"
  lang javascript
  file lib/redirect.js
  reject VYQL-SEC-052
  reject_review code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcrossRedirect
  code
  ```
  const { URL } = require('url');

  function removeMatchingHeaders(regex, headers) {
  	for (const header of Object.keys(headers)) {
  		if (regex.test(header)) {
  			delete headers[header];
  		}
  	}
  }

  function buildRedirect(response, current) {
  	const location = response.headers['location'];
  	if (!location) {
  		return null;
  	}

  	const target = new URL(location, current.url);
  	const options = {
  		headers: Object.assign({}, current.headers),
  		method: current.method,
  		maxRedirects: current.maxRedirects,
  		redirectCount: current.redirectCount + 1
  	};

  	if (new URL(current.url).host !== target.host) {
  		removeMatchingHeaders(/^(?:(?:proxy-)?authorization|cookie2?)$/i, options.headers);
  	}

  	return { url: target.toString(), options };
  }

  module.exports = { buildRedirect };
  ```

test "rank1455: a handler writing a Location header is emitting a redirect, not following one"
  lang javascript
  file lib/routes.js
  reject VYQL-SEC-052
